# Proposal: refute a claimed property

You are shown the documented rules of the checks and three properties someone claimed the code has after reading it. Do not read the code. Pick the claimed property this cycle's earlier proposals have not tested, and write ONE pair that would refute it if it is false: an input and a rewrite of the kind the claim names, and set expect to what the CLAIM says the answer does. If the machine disagrees with the claim, the claim was wrong; if it agrees, the claim survived one attempt. The commitments are a STRING whose content is JSON of the form {"kernel": "<id>", "input": "<the input text>", "rewritten": "<your rewritten text>", "expect": "moves" or "unchanged", "rewrite": "<one sentence naming what you changed>"}. The input and the rewritten text are instances written out in full, never descriptions. Do not repeat a pair shown among the earlier proposals. For the grounding kernels the input is one JSON object with the fields value, span and document, written out as a string; a line break inside a field is written as \n. The commitments also carry "claim": "<the claim you are testing, verbatim>".

## The problem (problem)
The checks under test are the conformance harness's own functions. Most seats are shown only their documented rules, not their code: how the harness recovers a JSON object from a reply, whether it had to recover it from prose, its response verdict, its refusal-phrase check, and its grounding matcher. The question: is there an input and a rewrite of it on which the rule, read as written, says one thing and the check, run by machine, does another? The executor runs the check on both texts and records what its answer did; nothing here decides anything, the record shows where a reading and the machine parted.

